  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Rajesh Subramaniam vs Food Safety Officer on 27 September, 2022 In The Court Of Mr. Dharmesh Sharma Principal District & Sessions Judge / Food Safety Appellate Tribunal; New Delhi District Patiala House Courts : New Delhi Fsat01/2022 Cnr No. Dlnd10014522022 In re: 1. Rajesh Subramaniam S/o Sh. Karpan Subramaniam R/o H.No. 455C, Sunlight Colony, New Delhi110014 2. Vijay Kumar S/o Sh. Surender Prasad R/o H.No. 24, Gali No.8, Babarpur, New Delhi110044 ........ Appellants Versus 1. Food Safety Officer Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi, 8th Floor, Mayur Bhawan, Shanker Market, Connaught Palace, New Delhi 2. Vineet Dhingra S/o Sh. Suresh Chand M/s. The Town House Cafe, (A unit of J.V. Restaurant Private Limited), N63, 64, Munshi Lal Building, Outer Circle Connaught Place, New Delhi110001 R/o S170/288, Jharera Village, New Delhi110010 Fsat01/2022 Rajesh Subramaniam & Ors. v. Fso Page 1 of 8 3. Rajeev Dass S/o Sh. Albert Wilson Dass M/s. The Town House Cafe, (A unit of J.V. Restaurant Private Limited), N63, 64, Munshi Lal Building, Outer Circle Connaught Place, New Delhi110001 R/o House No. 340, Bhai Parma Nand Colony, New Delhi 4. M/s. J.V. Restaurant Private Limited was having a restaurant in the name of Rajesh Subramaniam vs Food Safety Officer on 27 September, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/120781832/ 1 (\"The Town House Cafe\") Through Resolution Professional N63, 64, Munshi Lal Building, Outer Circle Connaught Place, New Delhi110001 .......Respondents Date of Institution : 21.02.2022 Date of hearing arguments : 19.09.2022 Date of Judgment : 27.09.2022",
    "facts": "whereby, the appellant have been imposed consolidated penalty of Rs. One Lakh for violation of Section 26 (2) (ii) and 27 (1) read with Section 3 (1) (i) of the FSS Act, punishable under Section 54 of the FSS Act. BRIEF FACTS: 2. In a nutshell, the proforma respondent No. 4 M/ s. J.V. Restaurant Private Limited is a Company and engaged in the business of running Restaurants in Delhi, under the name & style 'The Town House Cafe' having its registered office at N63/64, Munshi Lal Building, Outer Circle, Connaught Place, New Delhi and the respondent No.2 Sh. Vineet Dhingra was the FBOcumChef in the said Cafe whereas the appellants are the Directors of the respondent No.4. It is stated that on 09.03.2018 at about 2.46 p.m. Sh. Bal Mukund, the Food Safety Officer alongwith other officials visited the premises for inspection, which was fully co operated by the the respondent Nos. 2 and four portions of 'Prepared Dal Tadka' were bought for analysis, for which credit note of Rs. 1,502/ dated 09.03.2018 was given. It is stated that on 01.10.2018 a complaint was filed by the FSO against the appellants and the respondent Nos. 2 and 3 under the Rule 3.1.1.(3) of the Food Safety and Standards Rules, 2011 in the Court of Adjudicating Officer/Addl. District Magistrate concerned and a reply was filed by the appellants on 21.01.2019, who considered the Food Analyst report No. FSS/231/2018 dated 22.03.2018 and formed an opinion that the the samples were containing extraneous matter viz. vegetable oil in contravention of Section 26 (2) (ii), which led to the passing of the impugned order dated 18.11.2021. GROUNDS OF APPEAL: 3. The impugned order is assailed inter alia on the grounds that the Adjudicating Officer failed to correctly interpret Section 3 (1) (i) of FSS Act that would indicate that 'vegetable oil' does not come Rajesh Subramaniam vs Food Safety Officer on 27 September, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/120781832/ 2 under the definition of 'extraneous matter' as it is not carried from the raw materials or any packaging materials or process system used for its manufacture; and that the Food Safety Officer as well as Field Assistants had not followed the guidelines for sample collection in terms of Clause 1 of subRule 2.4.1 of Rule 2.4 of Food Safety and Standards Rules, 2011; and that the impugned order was passed without issuing any summons to the Chef i.e., respondent No.2; and that the Adjudicating Officer failed to appreciate that there was no question of commission of any cheating since edible vegetable oil is normally used to cook food and no food could be cooked in butter only, particularly when FSS Act no where prescribes recipe of 'Dal Tadka', that is required to be followed; and that the Adjudicating Officer failed to appreciate that proforma respondent No.4 viz. M/s. J.V. Restaurant Private Limited has gone into liquidation and Resolution Professional has been appointed subsequent to the winding up order and there is applicable moratorium by virtue of section 14 of the Insolvency & Bankruptcy Code. 4. On filing of the appeal, notice was ordered to be issued to the respondent and although no reply has been filed, the present appeal is vehemently opposed.",
    "arguments": "7. Further, section 26 of the FSS Act provides for the responsibility of the Food Business Operators and subSection (1) provides that every Food Business Operator shall ensure that articles of food satisfy requirement of the Act as also Rules and Regulations framed thereunder for each stages of production, processing, import, distribution and sale; and subSection (2) (ii) provides that no Food Business Operator shall himself or by any person on his behalf manufacture, store, sell, or distribution any article of food which is misbranded or sub standard or contains extraneous matter. Incidentally, Section 48(2) of the FSS Act provides that in determining whether any food is unsafe or injurious to health, regard shall be had to the normal conditions of use of the food by the consumer vide subclause (a) (i) and its handling at each stage of production, processing and distribution.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "7. Further, section 26 of the FSS Act provides for the responsibility of the Food Business Operators and subSection (1) provides that every Food Business Operator shall ensure that articles of food satisfy requirement of the Act as also Rules and Regulations framed thereunder for each stages of production, processing, import, distribution and sale; and subSection (2) (ii) provides that no Food Business Operator shall himself or by any person on his behalf manufacture, store, sell, or distribution any article of food which is misbranded or sub standard or contains extraneous matter. Incidentally, Section 48(2) of the FSS Act provides that in determining whether any food is unsafe or injurious to health, regard shall be had to the normal conditions of use of the food by the consumer vide subclause (a) (i) and its handling at each stage of production, processing and distribution."
  },
